/**

\page intro Introduction

Fl_Dock gives an FLTK application the docking panels of an IDE: a center,
such as a 3D view, with panels around it that the user drags to new places,
stacks as tabs, floats in windows of their own or inside the application's
window, and hides behind buttons on stripes along the window's edges.  The
application gives a default arrangement in a few calls and saves the
user's arrangement as text or in an Fl_Preferences.

It follows FLTK's conventions and license, needs nothing but FLTK 1.4 or
later, and is written for every platform FLTK supports.

\section intro_building Building and installing

Fl_Dock needs FLTK 1.4 or later and CMake 3.15 or later.

\code{.sh}
cmake -S . -B build -D CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=/path/to/fltk/install -D CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/path/to/install
cmake --build build
ctest --test-dir build
cmake --install build
\endcode

This builds the library \c fltk_dock, static unless \c BUILD_SHARED_LIBS is
set, and the test programs in \c test/, and installs the library, its
headers in \c FL/ and a CMake package.  Another project finds it with
\c find_package(Fl_Dock) and links \c fltk::dock.

The options are:

  - \c FL_DOCK_BUILD_TEST: build the test programs, on by default.
  - \c FL_DOCK_BUILD_HTML_DOCS: give the build a target \c html that makes
    this manual with Doxygen, off by default.
  - \c FL_DOCK_INSTALL_HTML_DOCS: install the manual made by the \c html
    target, off by default.

The version is in FL_DOCK_MAJOR_VERSION, FL_DOCK_MINOR_VERSION,
FL_DOCK_PATCH_VERSION and FL_DOCK_API_VERSION;
Fl_Dock::api_version() gives the version of the library an application is
linked with.

\section intro_using A first program

\code
#include <FL/Fl.H>
#include <FL/Fl_Double_Window.H>
#include <FL/Fl_Box.H>
#include <FL/Fl_Preferences.H>
#include <FL/Fl_Dock.H>
#include <FL/Fl_Dock_Panel.H>

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
  Fl_Double_Window *win = new Fl_Double_Window(800, 600, "Example");
  Fl_Dock *dock = new Fl_Dock(0, 0, 800, 600);
  win->end();
  win->resizable(dock);

  Fl_Group::current(0);
  dock->center(new Fl_Box(FL_DOWN_BOX, 0, 0, 400, 400, "view"));
  Fl_Dock_Panel *files = new Fl_Dock_Panel(0, 0, 200, 300, "Files");
  files->name("files");       // the name saved layouts know it by
  // ... the panel's widgets ...
  files->end();
  dock->add_panel(files, FL_DOCK_LEFT, 0, 0.25);

  Fl_Preferences prefs(Fl_Preferences::USER_L, "example.org", "example");
  dock->load_layout(prefs, "last");   // before show(): floating windows show with the window
  win->show(argc, argv);
  int r = Fl::run();
  dock->save_layout(prefs, "last");
  return r;
}
\endcode

Things worth knowing:

  - Unlike other groups, a dock does not become the current group: panels
    are given to it with Fl_Dock::add_panel(), and may be made anywhere.
  - A panel's name identifies it in saved layouts.  It is the label unless
    Fl_Dock_Panel::name() sets it; set it when labels are translated.  A dock
    refuses a panel with no name or with a name it already has.
  - The dock deletes its panels, open or closed, its center and its floating
    windows with itself.  Fl_Dock::remove_panel() gives a panel back, and
    deleting a panel is safe at any time, as for any FLTK child;
    Fl_Dock::center(0) gives the center back.
  - Restore a layout before showing the window, or after; floating windows
    are shown and hidden with the dock's window, so closing that window ends
    Fl::run().
  - The dock's callback is called for what the user does, not for the dock's
    functions, as Fl_Widget::when() asks; Fl_Dock::callback_panel() and
    Fl::callback_reason() say what changed.
  - The docks install an Fl::event_dispatch() function while any dock
    exists.  An application that sets its own afterwards must have it call
    the one it replaced; see Fl_Dock::dispatch().

\section intro_platforms Platforms

On Wayland a top-level window cannot learn where it is on the screen, so a
panel cannot be dragged from one top-level window into another there; the
tab menu's Float and Dock items move it instead.  Everything else, inset
and sliding windows included, works within the application's window as on
other platforms.

*/
